\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the 405{} Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration by Equation\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:accel}, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The right-hand axis is the same quantity as a fractional drift rate, $|\dot  \nu /\nu |=a/c$, which is the unit the literature states drift reach in. The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$ or 12.0\,nHz, which is also the median; it runs to 4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the 5{} TRAPPIST--1{} windows. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: the two most demanding known planets of any target, TRAPPIST--1 b{} (4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$, known to $\pm 3.1$ per cent and so equal to the widened ceiling within that) and GJ 581 e{} (2.27\,m\,s$^{-2}$, whose host is searched in coarse-channel windows only), and the equatorial rotation of a Jupiter, 2.2\,m\,s$^{-2}$ or 7.4\,nHz; Earth's own rotation and orbit, 0.034{} and 0.0059\,m\,s$^{-2}$, lie below the axis. \textbf  {Transmitters whose combined orbital and rotational acceleration exceeds the ceiling are not constrained by this search, and the ceiling is a budget they share}: a transmitter on the equator of a rotating giant spends 61{} per cent of it on spin alone. Nor are unbound transmitters, or any platform accelerating for its own reasons.}}{7}{figure.4.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{3}{7}{What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the \NWinA {} Class~A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration by Equation~\ref {eq:accel}, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The right-hand axis is the same quantity as a fractional drift rate, $|\dot \nu /\nu |=a/c$, which is the unit the literature states drift reach in. The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$ or \DriftCeilLo \,nHz, which is also the median; it runs to \AccelCeilMax \,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the \AccelNWide {} \AccelWideStar {} windows. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: the two most demanding known planets of any target, \AccelWorstName {} (\AccelWorstVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$, known to $\pm \TrpAmaxPct $ per cent and so equal to the widened ceiling within that) and \AccelSecondName {} (\AccelSecondVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$, whose host is searched in coarse-channel windows only), and the equatorial rotation of a Jupiter, \AccelJupRot \,m\,s$^{-2}$ or \AccelJupRotNHz \,nHz; Earth's own rotation and orbit, \AccelEarthRot {} and \AccelEarthOrb \,m\,s$^{-2}$, lie below the axis. \textbf {Transmitters whose combined orbital and rotational acceleration exceeds the ceiling are not constrained by this search, and the ceiling is a budget they share}: a transmitter on the equator of a rotating giant spends \AccelJupPct {} per cent of it on spin alone. Nor are unbound transmitters, or any platform accelerating for its own reasons}{figure.4.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}From a crossing to a confirmed signal}{7}{subsection.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
