\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Can two crossings be one carrier?} Every pair of crossings toward one star, in two execution blocks, lying within $\pm 833$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of each other in the star's frame -- the half-width of the median spectral window a repeat could be found in -- and carrying between them two independently fitted drifts and two block epochs measured from their own data: 13{} pairs with an unattributed member, of the 16{} inside that interval. Below the rule is the control, the 1{} of 23{} pairs of already attributed crossings for which a drift and an epoch were both recorded; it is a line that does not drift, so it shows the test accepting a pair at one frequency and not a drifting carrier. Stars are identified by position, so the two archival-tail crossings of HD\nobreakspace  {}14055{} pair with its catalogued ones. $\Delta \nu $ is the stellar-frame offset and $\dot  \nu _1$, $\dot  \nu _2$ the drifts fitted independently in the two blocks and carried into that frame. A carrier whose drift changes at a constant rate between the epochs would put the pair at $\Delta \nu =\frac  {1}{2}(\dot  \nu _1+\dot  \nu _2)\Delta t$; constant line-of-sight acceleration is the special case $\dot  \nu _1=\dot  \nu _2$, which 13{} of these pairs fail outright. \emph  {Miss} is how far the pair is from that prediction in units of the drift grid's resolution carried over $\Delta t$, which is also the resolution the comparison has on the mean of the two drifts; a dash means it exceeds the window searched, so the comparison has no power at all. The last column is the verdict under the weaker criterion of a trajectory whose drift stays inside the searched grid: \emph  {no} where no such trajectory joins the pair, \emph  {open} where the pair is not separable -- neither a confirmation nor an exclusion -- \emph  {yes} for the control row. The subscript $\star $ marks the star's frame, in which the drifts are given; the grid was stepped in the observatory's, and Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:edge} gives the rates in it. 1{} of the rates printed here, 4216\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ toward HD\nobreakspace  {}14055, is the outermost trial of its own window's grid: there the search reached the edge of the interval it covers, so the rate is a lower bound -- 3.66\,m\,s$^{-2}$ or more -- and every pair that uses it is marked \emph  {edge} rather than \emph  {open}. The exception is the row the criterion separates, whose verdict uses neither rate: that pair's own offset of 825\,MHz is 25{} times the 33\,MHz the ceiling permits over 2.23\,hours, whatever the two rates are. Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:edge} establishes the grid's edges and lists every edge-limited crossing.}}{18}{table.5.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:pairs}{{5}{18}{\textbf {Can two crossings be one carrier?} Every pair of crossings toward one star, in two execution blocks, lying within $\pm \WdHalfKms $\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of each other in the star's frame -- the half-width of the median spectral window a repeat could be found in -- and carrying between them two independently fitted drifts and two block epochs measured from their own data: \TjNPair {} pairs with an unattributed member, of the \TjNCandPair {} inside that interval. Below the rule is the control, the \TjNCandAttrTest {} of \TjNCandAttr {} pairs of already attributed crossings for which a drift and an epoch were both recorded; it is a line that does not drift, so it shows the test accepting a pair at one frequency and not a drifting carrier. Stars are identified by position, so the two archival-tail crossings of \TjDeepStar {} pair with its catalogued ones. $\Delta \nu $ is the stellar-frame offset and $\dot \nu _1$, $\dot \nu _2$ the drifts fitted independently in the two blocks and carried into that frame. A carrier whose drift changes at a constant rate between the epochs would put the pair at $\Delta \nu =\frac {1}{2}(\dot \nu _1+\dot \nu _2)\Delta t$; constant line-of-sight acceleration is the special case $\dot \nu _1=\dot \nu _2$, which \TjNUneqDrift {} of these pairs fail outright. \emph {Miss} is how far the pair is from that prediction in units of the drift grid's resolution carried over $\Delta t$, which is also the resolution the comparison has on the mean of the two drifts; a dash means it exceeds the window searched, so the comparison has no power at all. The last column is the verdict under the weaker criterion of a trajectory whose drift stays inside the searched grid: \emph {no} where no such trajectory joins the pair, \emph {open} where the pair is not separable -- neither a confirmation nor an exclusion -- \emph {yes} for the control row. The subscript $\star $ marks the star's frame, in which the drifts are given; the grid was stepped in the observatory's, and Table~\ref {tab:edge} gives the rates in it. \EgNPairDriftEdge {} of the rates printed here, \EgStarDriftHzS \,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ toward \EgStarALosHiStar , is the outermost trial of its own window's grid: there the search reached the edge of the interval it covers, so the rate is a lower bound -- \EgStarALosHi \,m\,s$^{-2}$ or more -- and every pair that uses it is marked \emph {edge} rather than \emph {open}. The exception is the row the criterion separates, whose verdict uses neither rate: that pair's own offset of \EgExclOffMHz \,MHz is \EgExclFac {} times the \EgExclReachMHz \,MHz the ceiling permits over \EgExclDtH \,hours, whatever the two rates are. Appendix~\ref {sec:edge} establishes the grid's edges and lists every edge-limited crossing}{table.5.5}{}}
