\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The survey ledger, part two: events, dispositions and confirmation.} Every headline count in this paper is read from here, and \textbf  {the itemisation accompanies this paper as data rather than as part of the article}, machine-readable and carrying all 21{} rows, which is why the numbering here is not consecutive. Three counts are 52{} over three different sets -- the Class\nobreakspace  {}A crossings of the adopted ledger, those of the per-window extraction catalogue (\S  \ref  {sec:sample}), and the quality-passing crossings. The first two differ on 6{} rows, where the statistic read over a narrower field falls on the other side of the trigger from the whole-primary-beam reading the ledger adopts, in both directions (Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {app:falsealarm}); the flag the accompanying table carries is the ledger's, 50{} of them in the census. The last two hold the same rows, because the one block failing the quality criterion is also the survey's only block with a coarse-channel crossing. Row 3.1.1 is what this paper means by confirmation and the only such count it quotes: it applies the criterion of \S  \ref  {sec:summary}, a different observing session and more than a day, at a frequency the first block covered. Row 2.2.1 is decomposed system by system in Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {app:falsealarm}.}}{29}{table.A.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:counts}{{A2}{29}{\textbf {The survey ledger, part two: events, dispositions and confirmation.} Every headline count in this paper is read from here, and \textbf {the itemisation accompanies this paper as data rather than as part of the article}, machine-readable and carrying all \MtCountRows {} rows, which is why the numbering here is not consecutive. Three counts are \MtCrossA {} over three different sets -- the Class~A crossings of the adopted ledger, those of the per-window extraction catalogue (\S \ref {sec:sample}), and the quality-passing crossings. The first two differ on \FrNFlag {} rows, where the statistic read over a narrower field falls on the other side of the trigger from the whole-primary-beam reading the ledger adopts, in both directions (Appendix~\ref {app:falsealarm}); the flag the accompanying table carries is the ledger's, \MtCrossACen {} of them in the census. The last two hold the same rows, because the one block failing the quality criterion is also the survey's only block with a coarse-channel crossing. Row 3.1.1 is what this paper means by confirmation and the only such count it quotes: it applies the criterion of \S \ref {sec:summary}, a different observing session and more than a day, at a frequency the first block covered. Row 2.2.1 is decomposed system by system in Appendix~\ref {app:falsealarm}}{table.A.2}{}}
